WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:05.962
Erin did not want to put on the bright blue raincoat that the lady at the kiosk handed to her.

2
00:00:05.963 --> 00:00:09.406
"Why do I have to wear this?" she asked.

3
00:00:09.407 --> 00:00:15.729
Erin’s brother, Colin, smiled and said, "You’ll be sorry if you don’t put it on, trust me!"

4
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:23.213
They were in the lineup to board the Maid of the Mist , the boat that would take them on a tour beneath Niagara

5
00:00:23.214 --> 00:00:28.276
Falls. It was Erin’s first time visiting the falls and she couldn’t wait to see

6
00:00:28.277 --> 00:00:34.738
them. Erin could hear the roar of the water ahead and, as she peeked over her brother’s shoulder, she saw

7
00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:37.605
them! The view of the falls was

8
00:00:37.606 --> 00:00:43.202
astounding. The water from the Niagara River was flowing fast, thundering over a high

9
00:00:43.203 --> 00:00:51.918
cliff. A giant cloud of mist rose from the base of the falls, past the top of the cliffs. Erin was both excited and scared.

10
00:00:51.919 --> 00:00:59.863
She joined the rest of the tourists and climbed aboard the Maid of the Mist , where she was greeted by a friendly tour

11
00:00:59.864 --> 00:01:06.800
operator. Niagara Falls was formed over ten thousand years ago, when the area was covered by thick

12
00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:12.941
ice. As the ice melted, a few large glaciers began moving north, creating deep gouges in the

13
00:01:12.942 --> 00:01:24.443
earth. These gouges formed the Niagara Escarpment, which is a ridge of cliffs and hills that follows a broad curve from New York State, through the Canadian province of Ontario, to

14
00:01:24.444 --> 00:01:30.174
Wisconsin. The Great Lakes were formed at the same time and are the largest group of freshwater lakes in the

15
00:01:30.175 --> 00:01:41.611
world. The water from the lakes created the Niagara River, and when the enormous volume of water flowed over the cliffs of the Niagara Escarpment, it created the high waterfalls of Niagara

16
00:01:41.612 --> 00:01:47.659
Falls. The falls look different and are in a different location today than when they were first

17
00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:56.254
formed. The great force of the Niagara River erodes the rock of the riverbed and the cliffs, causing the falls to recede a little further upstream every

18
00:01:56.255 --> 00:02:02.880
year. Since their formation, the falls have retreated several kilometers upstream from the

19
00:02:02.881 --> 00:02:11.739
escarpment. The Niagara Falls are actually three different waterfalls: the American Falls, the Bridal Veil Falls, and the Horseshoe

20
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:23.615
Falls. The largest and most spectacular are the Horseshoe Falls, named because the water has eroded them into a shape like the letter "U," which resembles the metal shoe nailed to a horse’s

21
00:02:23.616 --> 00:02:28.822
hoof. The Niagara Falls are located on the border between the United States and

22
00:02:28.823 --> 00:02:34.727
Canada. The Horseshoe Falls are on the Canadian side, and the other two on the American side.

23
00:02:34.728 --> 00:02:42.100
Although Niagara Falls is not the highest waterfall in the world, it is one of the widest and certainly the most famous.

24
00:02:42.101 --> 00:02:45.990
The falls drop about 52 meters at their highest point.

25
00:02:45.991 --> 00:02:53.736
The Horseshoe Falls are 792 meters wide and the American Falls, 323 meters wide.

26
00:02:53.737 --> 00:03:03.379
During the summer months, when the Niagara River is at its peak, more then 2,650,000 liters of water pass over the cliffs every second.

27
00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:14.023
Victoria Falls in Africa and Angel Falls in South America may be higher, but the magnificence of Niagara Falls makes them one of the most beautiful in the world.

28
00:03:14.024 --> 00:03:20.379
The area around the falls was first populated by the Clovis people, soon after the falls were formed.

29
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:27.033
These ancient ancestors of the Native people of North America hunted the woolly mammoths at the end of the Ice Age.

30
00:03:27.034 --> 00:03:33.008
Traveling north, they spread throughout the continent and built small huts around the Niagara River.

31
00:03:33.009 --> 00:03:38.021
One thousand years ago, the Iroquois people settled in the same lands.

32
00:03:38.022 --> 00:03:42.692
They built small villages and cultivated the fertile land around the falls.

33
00:03:42.693 --> 00:03:47.975
The falls were so important to the Iroquois that they often appear in their stories and legends.

34
00:03:47.976 --> 00:03:57.041
In one Iroquois legend, a beautiful young woman was in love with the powerful god of thunder, who lived in a cave behind the Horseshoe Falls.

35
00:03:57.042 --> 00:04:03.070
Rather than marry the man chosen by her father, she threw herself over the falls to be with her lover.

36
00:04:03.071 --> 00:04:08.441
The name "Niagara" derives from an Iroquois word meaning "the channel."

37
00:04:08.442 --> 00:04:13.337
The first European explorers arrived at the beginning of the seventeenth century. Louis

38
00:04:13.338 --> 00:04:23.321
Hennepin, a French missionary, was the first explorer to write about the falls and described them in his diary as "an incredible waterfall which has no equal." As

39
00:04:23.322 --> 00:04:28.907
more settlers began arriving from Europe, the fame of Niagara Falls spread. Napoleon’s

40
00:04:28.908 --> 00:04:34.947
brother traveled from France to visit the falls with his bride in the early nineteenth century. By

41
00:04:34.948 --> 00:04:39.427
the middle of the century, tourism was the main industry in the area. Niagara

42
00:04:39.428 --> 00:04:46.537
Falls was considered one of nature’s greatest marvels and attracted many adventurers looking to build their fame. In

43
00:04:46.538 --> 00:04:55.913
1859, the French tightrope walker Charles Blondin crossed from one side to the other on a three-inch-thick rope stretched high above the gorge. In

44
00:04:55.914 --> 00:05:03.008
1901, a 63-year-old school teacher named Annie Taylor traveled over the falls in a wooden barrel. She

45
00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:08.404
emerged practically unscathed, exclaiming,"Did I go over the falls yet?" Countless

46
00:05:08.405 --> 00:05:17.572
other daredevils attempted to travel over the falls in boats or barrels; swimmers tried to swim over the falls or to cross the gorge at their base. Most,

47
00:05:17.573 --> 00:05:23.267
however, didn’t survive, and daredevil stunts were outlawed in 1912. Today,

48
00:05:23.268 --> 00:05:27.885
the falls are even more popular as a tourist destination. More

49
00:05:27.886 --> 00:05:32.891
than twelve million tourists from all over the world visit Niagara Falls each year. One

50
00:05:32.892 --> 00:05:42.205
of the most popular tourist excursions is a trip on the Maid of the Mist ; these boat rides have been in operation since 1846.

51
00:05:42.206 --> 00:05:51.691
The boats travel up the river to the foot of the falls, where their passengers, wearing raincoats and drenched by the mist, can experience the splendor of the falls close up.

52
00:05:51.692 --> 00:05:56.977
Tourists can also take a cable car that crosses the gorge to see the falls from above. They

53
00:05:56.978 --> 00:06:03.192
can even walk through a tunnel in the cliff and see the wall of crashing water from behind. Over

54
00:06:03.193 --> 00:06:10.319
the years, famous people have continued to visit the falls, which have provided the setting for countless movies. Marilyn

55
00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:16.491
Monroe made them a popular honeymoon destination in her 1953 movie, Niagara .

56
00:06:16.492 --> 00:06:23.410
The falls are spectacular at all times of the day, but especially at night, when they are illuminated by colored lights.

57
00:06:23.411 --> 00:06:29.520
As their boat approached the foot of the falls, Erin could feel the mist falling on her like heavy rain.

58
00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:32.694
She was happy she was wearing the blue raincoat.

59
00:06:32.695 --> 00:06:37.472
The roaring water plummeted in front of her, and she could feel it like thunder.

60
00:06:37.473 --> 00:06:39.909
Her heart pounded with excitement.

61
00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:45.281
In seeing Niagara Falls, she truly felt the power and beauty of nature.

